  "abstract": {
    "value": "The excessive emission of $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ has intensified global environmental and energy-related challenges, including climate change, carbon-cycle imbalance, and increasing pressure on sustainable energy systems. Converting $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ into value-added fuels and chemicals is therefore considered an attractive strategy for simultaneously mitigating greenhouse gas emissions and realizing carbon resource utilization (Sathyanadh et al., 2026; Boubaker et al., 2025; Kumaravel et al., 2020; Vadivel et al., 2024; Dang et al., 2024; Vu et al., 2019). Among different $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ conversion routes, photocatalytic $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ reduction is particularly promising because it can directly utilize solar energy to drive chemical transformation under mild conditions, offering a sustainable route for solar-to-chemical energy conversion (Bellardita et al., 2021; Inoue et al., 1979; Tjandra et al.,. 2018).",
